EXT. TREEHOUSE - BACKYARD - NIGHT

Rain drums on a sheet-metal roof. A lantern swings from a rafter. The treehouse is just big enough for three, and today it holds all of them: COLM DOYLE, 40s, in a damp raincoat, bent double under the low ceiling; ROSIE, 8, in pajamas and a rain slicker, clutching a stuffed heron; and GRANNY MAUDE, 70s, who somehow climbed the rope ladder and sits on an upturned bucket knitting.

COLM
The storm will pass.

ROSIE
You said that an hour ago.

COLM
It's a very big storm.

Thunder cracks. Rosie ducks and burrows into Maude's cardigan. Maude does not look up from her needles.

MAUDE
Rosemary. Sit up. The heron wants air.

ROSIE
He's scared.

MAUDE
He's a heron. He's seen worse.

Colm checks his phone. No signal. He pockets it and crouches by the door.

COLM
We should go in.

ROSIE
No!

COLM
It's flooding the lawn.

ROSIE
You said we'd sleep out here. You promised.

COLM
I promised dry.

Wind hammers the roof. A branch bangs the wall. The lantern swings, and Maude and Colm both reach for it at once, their hands closing on the same handle. Rosie giggles. Colm lets go. Maude hangs the lantern back on its hook.

MAUDE
Colm. Sit.

He sits. The floor tilts a degree. All three lean the same way and stop.

MAUDE (CONT'D)
When your father built this, he said it'd survive anything.

COLM
He was building it for me.

MAUDE
He built it for himself and told you it was for you.

Colm laughs, a short surprised breath. Rosie looks between them.

ROSIE
Was it scary then?

COLM
Not once he was in it.

Another crack. The roof rattles. The treehouse sways. A loud creak, and the whole structure shudders, and Colm throws an arm across Rosie as Maude plants both feet. A long moment. The wood settles. The rain eases to a patter.

ROSIE
(muffled in his coat)
We're still here.

MAUDE
Of course we are.

Colm lets out a breath. He kisses the top of Rosie's head. Maude counts a row of stitches. The heron's beak pokes out of Rosie's slicker.
